  "cloneCompositionStatement": "OID-keyed content caching composes with the API read paths (T0/T1): a cache hit skips a REST request or shrinks a GraphQL batch, which is exactly what the warm legs measure. On the clone paths (T2a/T2c) the unit's cost is dominated by whole-branch pack transfer, which an OID-keyed CONTENT cache does not reduce — a warm T2c leg still clones the branch and saves only local cat-file reads (microseconds each). What git natively provides in this direction is incremental object transfer against a PERSISTED prior store (fetch negotiation); production's fresh-clone-per-unit design has no persisted store, so that reuse is a different architecture (a shared object store), not a cache layer over the measured drivers."
